INT. BRIDAL SHOP - FITTING ROOM - DAY

A velvet-curtained cubicle, mirrors on three walls. A wedding gown on a hanger. ADELAIDE VOSSBERG, twenties, in a slip, stands on the dais, arms out. Her mother GERTRUDE, fifties, kneels with a mouthful of pins.

GERTRUDE
Stand still.

ADELAIDE
I am standing still.

A WOMAN in a sensible coat peers through the curtain. Sixties, hair in a bun, hands wringing. Neither of them has seen her before.

WOMAN
Pardon me. Is this the Vossberg fitting?

GERTRUDE
It is. And you are?

WOMAN
Just someone who used to know the groom's father.

Gertrude's pins clatter to the floor. Adelaide turns on the dais.

ADELAIDE
Mother?

GERTRUDE
(to the Woman, carefully)
You'd better come in.

The Woman steps inside, letting the curtain fall. She holds out a gloved hand.

WOMAN
Tillie. Tillie Marchetti.

GERTRUDE
(not taking it)
I know who you are.

TILLIE
Then you know why I'm here.

TILLIE
(gently, to Adelaide)
He asked me to give you something, dear.

Adelaide looks from one to the other in the three mirrors, six faces, six mothers.

ADELAIDE
Will someone please tell me what's going on?
